```javascript
const PAGE_SIZE = 100;

export function mountEvalTable(root, rows) {
  let view = rows;
  let page = 0;
  let desc = false;

  const table = document.createElement('table');
  table.innerHTML =
    '<thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Text</th><th>Gold</th><th>Pred</th>' +
    '<th><button type="button">Score</button></th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody>';
  const tbody = table.tBodies[0];

  const makeButton = (text) => {
    const b = document.createElement('button');
    b.type = 'button';
    b.textContent = text;
    return b;
  };
  const prev = makeButton('Prev');
  const next = makeButton('Next');
  const label = document.createElement('span');
  const pager = document.createElement('div');
  pager.append(prev, label, next);

  const lastPage = () => Math.max(0, Math.ceil(view.length / PAGE_SIZE) - 1);

  function render() {
    const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
    const start = page * PAGE_SIZE;
    for (const r of view.slice(start, start + PAGE_SIZE)) {
      const tr = document.createElement('tr');
      for (const v of [r.id, r.text, r.gold, r.pred, r.score.toFixed(3)]) {
        const td = document.createElement('td');
        td.textContent = v;
        tr.append(td);
      }
      frag.append(tr);
    }
    tbody.replaceChildren(frag);
    label.textContent = ` Page ${page + 1} / ${lastPage() + 1} (${view.length} rows) `;
    prev.disabled = page === 0;
    next.disabled = page >= lastPage();
  }

  table.querySelector('thead button').addEventListener('click', () => {
    desc = !desc;
    view = rows.slice().sort((a, b) => (desc ? b.score - a.score : a.score - b.score));
    page = 0;
    render();
  });
  prev.addEventListener('click', () => { page -= 1; render(); });
  next.addEventListener('click', () => { page += 1; render(); });

  root.replaceChildren(pager, table);
  render();
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Limit DOM size** (reduces E on the client): only 100 rows exist in the DOM at a time, not 30k. Sorting runs on the data array, so it still covers all rows and only the visible page is rebuilt. In Green Metrics Tool browser benchmarks (30 page loads of a 10,000-row table), the paginated plain `<table>` used ~457 J and the one-by-one wrapped version ~1036 J, about 56% less. Your row count and markup differ, so measure your own page.
- **Plain cells, one fragment** (reduces E): one `<td>` per value with no wrapper elements, and each page is built in a `DocumentFragment` and inserted once.
- `textContent` is used for the sample text, so model input containing HTML is never parsed as markup.

We launch a new landing page for our time-tracking SaaS next week. Static HTML on Netlify. This is the only JS file, loaded with `<script src="/js/landing.js" defer>`. It's about 9 KB right now, with comments and some console.logs from debugging.

Please review it before launch. Requirements:
