  <img src="img/start-line.jpg" alt="Runners at the start line" loading="lazy">
  <h1>Lakeside Marathon 2027</h1>
</header>

<section id="gallery">
  <h2>Last year</h2>
  <img src="img/g01.jpg" alt="Finish arch">
  <img src="img/g02.jpg" alt="Water station">
  <img src="img/g03.jpg" alt="Kids run">
  <img src="img/g04.jpg" alt="Medal table">
  <img src="img/g05.jpg" alt="Lake view">
  <img src="img/g06.jpg" alt="Volunteers">
</section>

<section id="startlist">
  <h2>Start list</h2>
  <input id="q" placeholder="Search name or bib">
  <div id="list"></div>
</section>

<script>
fetch('data/runners.json').then(r => r.json()).then(runners => {
  const list = document.getElementById('list');
  function render(items) {
    list.innerHTML = '';
    items.forEach(r => {
      const row = document.createElement('div');
      row.className = 'row';
      row.innerHTML =
        '<div class="cell"><span class="bib">' + r.bib + '</span></div>' +
        '<div class="cell"><span class="name">' + r.name + '</span></div>' +
        '<div class="cell"><span class="club">' + r.club + '</span></div>' +
        '<div class="cell"><span class="wave">' + r.wave + '</span></div>';
      list.appendChild(row);
    });
  }
  render(runners);
  document.getElementById('q').addEventListener('input', e => {
    const t = e.target.value.toLowerCase();
    render(runners.filter(r => r.name.toLowerCase().includes(t) || String(r.bib) === t));
  });
});
</script>
```

**Workload:** web (public landing page, mostly mobile)

#### [Security] Runner data is inserted as HTML
- **Where:** `row.innerHTML = ... r.name ... r.club`
- **Issue:** a name or club containing `<script>` or an `onerror=` attribute runs in every visitor's browser. Runner names usually come from a sign-up form, so treat them as untrusted.
- **Fix:** set `textContent` on each cell (see the code below).

#### [High] 6,000 rows with 3 elements each in the live DOM
- **Where:** `render(runners)` and the `.row` / `.cell` / `<span>` structure
- **Issue:** every visitor builds about 24,000 elements, and every keystroke in the search box throws them away and builds them again. The wrapper `div` and `span` around each value add nodes and no meaning.
- **Fix:** show 100 rows per page, use a plain `<table>`, build each page in a `DocumentFragment`, and filter first and then paginate. Search still covers all 6,000 runners.
- **Impact:** reduces E on the client for every page view, and memory on phones. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, a 10,000-row table with wrappers around every cell used about 56% less CPU energy when paginated to 100 rows per page as a plain table. Your list is smaller, so expect a smaller gap.

#### [Medium] The hero image is lazy-loaded
- **Where:** `<img src="img/start-line.jpg" ... loading="lazy">`
- **Issue:** it is the first thing visitors see. Lazy loading delays it, which goes against your "hero must show fast" requirement.
- **Fix:** remove `loading="lazy"` and add `fetchpriority="high"`.

#### [Medium] Gallery images load eagerly
- **Where:** the six `<img>` tags in `#gallery`
- **Issue:** they are below the fold, but all of them are fetched and decoded on load.
- **Fix:** add `loading="lazy"`, plus `width` and `height` so the layout does not jump.
- **Impact:** reduces network and E for visitors who never scroll that far. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, lazy loading below-the-fold photos used about 12% less energy on page loads without scrolling. When visitors scrolled to the end, there was no difference. It does not hurt, but it is not a big win for visitors who read the whole page.

```html
