UI bundle (html/js/css/svg) is baked into the firmware image at build time. /api/telemetry returns 20-200 KB JSON, different on every call.

```text
 server {
     listen 8080;
     server_name _;

     root /opt/gw/ui;
     index index.html;

+    gzip_static on;
+    gzip on;
+    gzip_comp_level 1;
+    gzip_min_length 1024;
+    gzip_vary on;
+    gzip_types application/json text/css application/javascript image/svg+xml;
+
     location / {
         try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
     }
```

Create the `.gz` files in the firmware image build, next to the originals:

```bash
find /opt/gw/ui -type f \( -name '*.html' -o -name '*.js' -o -name '*.css' -o -name '*.svg' \) \
    -exec gzip -k -9 {} +
```

`text/html` is always compressed by `gzip on`, so it is not in `gzip_types`. The `/api/` block needs no change. Its JSON is compressed by the server-level `gzip on`.

**Green Notes**
- **Precompressed UI files** (reduces E, network): the bundle is fixed at image build time, so it is compressed once with `gzip_static` and not on every request. In Green Metrics Tool measurements of nginx serving text assets, compression sent about 10× fewer bytes. Compressing on every request cost about 4× the CPU energy of serving precompressed files. Level 9 is fine because it runs once per build.
- **Low level for the telemetry JSON** (reduces network): the response changes on every call, so it cannot be precompressed. Level 1 keeps the per-request CPU work small on a 2-core device. The cost is a slightly larger payload than higher levels.
- **`gzip_min_length 1024`**: tiny responses skip compression, because the CPU work is not worth the few bytes saved.

