*Not applied:* a custom brand font. If you want one later, use one or two WOFF2 files with a Latin subset, not full TTF sets.

Hello, we are small webshop (about 40 products pages, cart, checkout) and next week is big sale. Our devops colegue say nginx use too much CPU on the sale days and I think maybe we should switch off the compression complete, so server do less work and use less energy. How much energy we save with this? This is our config now:

```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name shop.example.com;

    root /srv/shop/dist;

    gzip on;
    gzip_comp_level 5;
    gzip_types text/css application/javascript application/json image/svg+xml;

    location /assets/ {
        expires 1y;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
    }

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
    }

    location / {
        try_files $uri /index.html;
    }
}
```

Don't switch it off. Turning it off would save server CPU, but you would pay for it in bytes sent to your customers.

In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, nginx served 2,000 requests for text assets (HTML, CSS, JS, JSON):

- **No compression:** ~7.3 J of CPU energy, 253 MB transferred.
- **gzip level 5 on every request** (like your config): ~54 J, 29 MB transferred.
- **Precompressed files with `gzip_static`:** ~14 J, 25 MB transferred.

Compression sent about 10× fewer bytes. Compressing on every request cost about 4× the CPU energy of precompressed files. The test ran on a local network, where moving bytes was almost free. So it shows only the CPU side. On real customer connections, and especially on mobile, the 10× fewer bytes is the part that counts.

The better change is to compress your static files once at build time and keep per-request compression only for the API:

```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name shop.example.com;

    root /srv/shop/dist;

    gzip on;
    gzip_comp_level 5;
    gzip_vary on;
    gzip_types text/css application/javascript application/json image/svg+xml;

    location /assets/ {
        gzip_static on;
        expires 1y;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
    }

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
    }

    location / {
        try_files $uri /index.html;
    }
}
```

In your build, run `gzip -k` on every `.js`, `.css`, `.json` and `.svg` file in `dist/assets`. This writes a `.gz` file next to each original. nginx then sends the `.gz` file and does no compression work for these requests.

- Your API answers are generated per request, so they cannot be precompressed. Keep `gzip on` for them.
- Precompressed files still cost a little more than no compression, because the browser has to decompress them. That is the normal price for the smaller transfer.

I can't give you a number for the sale days. It depends on your traffic and file sizes, and the benchmark figures should not be scaled up. To measure it, run the same set of requests against both configs and compare CPU time with `perf stat` or `time` on the nginx workers. A Green Metrics Tool usage scenario gives you energy directly.

hi, i am not sure what is wrong but our landing page feel slow on the small server and CPU go up when we have campaign traffic. the agency make this nginx config. can you look?

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name launch.example.com;

    root /var/www/launch;
    index index.html;

    gzip on;
    gzip_comp_level 9;
    gzip_vary on;
    gzip_types text/css application/javascript application/json image/svg+xml image/jpeg image/png video/mp4;

    brotli on;
    brotli_comp_level 11;
    brotli_types text/html text/css application/javascript image/svg+xml image/jpeg image/png;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }

    location /assets/ {
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
    }
}
```

**Workload:** web (static landing page, bursty campaign traffic)

#### [High] Maximum-effort compression on every request
- **Where:** `brotli_comp_level 11;` and `gzip_comp_level 9;`
- **Issue:** nginx compresses the same unchanged files again for every visitor, and it uses the slowest settings to do it. Brotli at quality 11 is built for files you compress once. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, brotli at quality 11 used about 45× the CPU energy of gzip at its default level when compressing 100 MB of text. That is likely your CPU spike during campaigns.
- **Fix:** compress the files once at build time, and let nginx send the ready files. Your files are static, so nothing is lost.
- **Impact:** reduces E on the server for every request. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, nginx serving text assets with per-request gzip (level 5) used about 4× the CPU energy of serving precompressed files. Compression sent about 10× fewer bytes in both cases, so keep it on for real networks.

#### [Medium] Images and video are in the compress lists
- **Where:** `image/jpeg`, `image/png`, `video/mp4` in `gzip_types` and `brotli_types`
- **Issue:** JPEG, PNG and MP4 are already compressed. Compressing them again burns CPU and saves almost no bytes.
- **Fix:** list only text types: HTML, CSS, JS, JSON and SVG.

#### [Low] Both gzip and brotli run on the fly
- **Where:** the `gzip` and `brotli` blocks
- **Issue:** with prebuilt files, nginx does not need to compress anything itself. If you keep a fallback for files that were not precompressed, a moderate gzip level is enough.

**Fix: config**

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name launch.example.com;

    root /var/www/launch;
    index index.html;

    gzip_static on;
    brotli_static on;
    gzip_vary on;

    # fallback for files without a precompressed copy
    gzip on;
    gzip_comp_level 5;
    gzip_types text/css application/javascript application/json image/svg+xml;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }

    location /assets/ {
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
    }
}
```

**Fix: run once after each build**

```bash
find /var/www/launch -type f \( -name '*.html' -o -name '*.css' -o -name '*.js' -o -name '*.json' -o -name '*.svg' \) \
    -exec gzip -k -9 -f {} + \
    -exec brotli -k -q 11 -f {} +
```

Max levels are fine here, because each file is compressed only once. `brotli_static` needs the ngx_brotli module, and `gzip_static` needs the gzip static module, so check both are in your nginx build. Rerun the command on every deploy, or visitors will get stale `.gz` and `.br` files.

