Only the current hour's file still changes. Every closed hour can be compressed once and served as a `.gz` from then on.

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name logs.internal;

    root /var/log/collected;

    allow 10.20.0.0/16;
    deny  all;

    location / {
        autoindex on;
        autoindex_exact_size off;
        autoindex_localtime on;

        # nginx's mime.types does not know .log, so serve it as text
        types {
            text/plain log;
        }
        default_type application/octet-stream;
        charset utf-8;

        # closed hours: send the .gz written once by the cron job below
        gzip_static on;

        # the hour that is still being written: compress on the fly, cheapest level
        gzip on;
        gzip_comp_level 1;
        gzip_types text/plain;
        gzip_vary on;
    }
}
```

Cron job that precompresses each hour after it closes:

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# /usr/local/bin/compress-closed-hours.sh
# crontab: 5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/compress-closed-hours.sh
set -euo pipefail
shopt -s nullglob

# use date -u if fluent-bit names the files in UTC
current="$(date +%Y-%m-%d-%H).log"

for f in /var/log/collected/*/*.log; do
    [[ ${f##*/} == "$current" ]] && continue
    [[ -e $f.gz ]] && continue
    # write to a temp file first, so nginx never serves a half-written .gz
    gzip -c -- "$f" > "$f.gz.tmp"
    mv -- "$f.gz.tmp" "$f.gz"
done
```

`gzip_static` only sends the `.gz` when the client sends `Accept-Encoding: gzip`. Browsers do this automatically. With curl, use `curl --compressed -O http://logs.internal/web-01/2026-10-24-13.log`. Without `--compressed`, curl gets the uncompressed file. The `.log` files stay next to the `.gz` files, so those clients still work.

**Green Notes**
- **Precompress closed hours** (reduces E): each file is compressed once by cron. After that, every download, including the repeated ones during an incident, gets the ready `.gz`. In Green Metrics Tool measurements of nginx serving text assets, gzip on every request cost about 4× the CPU energy of serving precompressed files.
- **Keep compression on** (reduces network): in the same measurement, compression sent about 10× fewer bytes for text. Over your VPN, the transfer is the slow part, and log text compresses well.
- **On-the-fly gzip only for the current hour, at level 1** (reduces E): this file is still growing, so it cannot be precompressed. Level 1 keeps the per-request CPU cost low and still shrinks plain text a lot.

