The `.log` files download because nginx does not know the `.log` type by default. The config below fixes that. It also serves compressed copies that the pipeline creates once, so nginx does not compress on every request.

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name ci-artifacts.internal;

    root /srv/ci-artifacts;
    autoindex on;

    include mime.types;
    types {
        text/plain log;
    }

    # Serve file.html.gz, file.log.gz, ... if they exist
    gzip_static on;

    # Fallback for files that were not precompressed
    gzip on;
    gzip_vary on;
    gzip_comp_level 2;
    gzip_min_length 1024;
    gzip_types text/plain text/css application/javascript application/json application/xml;
}
```

Add this step at the end of the Jenkins job, after the files are copied. It compresses each text file once:

```bash
find "$ARTIFACT_DIR" -type f \
    \( -name '*.html' -o -name '*.css' -o -name '*.js' -o -name '*.json' -o -name '*.xml' -o -name '*.log' \) \
    -exec gzip -9 -k -f {} +
```

`$ARTIFACT_DIR` is the folder of the current build, for example `/srv/ci-artifacts/<job>/<build number>`. Keep the originals (`-k`) so clients without gzip support still work. If a file is changed later, run the step again so the `.gz` copy is not stale.

**Green Notes**
- **Precompressed text files** (reduces E, network): a build's reports never change after the copy, so compress them once in the pipeline and let `gzip_static` serve the result. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks with nginx, compressing on every request cost about 4× the CPU energy of serving precompressed files. Compression sent about 10× fewer bytes for text assets. That test ran on a local network, so it shows the CPU side only. Your high CPU is most likely per-request compression of big coverage pages, which this removes.
- **Cheap fallback** (reduces E): `gzip_comp_level 2` keeps per-request work small for any file that was not precompressed.
- *Not applied:* compression for images or already-compressed files such as `.zip`. They gain nothing and only cost CPU.

