Add to the view's stylesheet. The speed matches your current one: one 10 px dash period every 0.5 s, moving in the same direction.

```css
.edge--flowing {
  animation: edge-flow 0.5s linear infinite;
}

@keyframes edge-flow {
  to { stroke-dashoffset: -10; }
}
```

One behavior change: each edge now starts its own cycle when it gets the class. It no longer joins a shared phase, so edges that start at different times are slightly out of step.

**What it saves**

Expect a modest saving per screen, not a big one. In Green Metrics Tool browser benchmarks, a 20-second animation in a real browser used ~161 J of CPU energy with JavaScript `requestAnimationFrame` and style updates, and ~145 J with CSS `@keyframes`, about 10% less. That was one simple animation on a test page. I can't turn it into a figure for 40 screens running all day.

What drives the saving in your case:
- **Per-frame JS goes away.** Today every frame runs `tick`, a `querySelectorAll`, and one inline style write per flowing edge, plus the style recalculation those writes trigger. With CSS, the browser drives the animation itself.
- **Paint stays.** `stroke-dashoffset` is not a compositor-only property, so the edges are still repainted every frame in both versions. That part of the cost doesn't change.
- **Screen-hours.** The saving only builds up while the view is visible and tasks are running. Browsers already throttle both rAF and CSS animations in background tabs.

**How to measure it**
1. Quick proxy: open the view with your usual number of running edges. Record a few seconds in the Firefox Profiler or the Chrome Performance panel for each version, then compare main-thread scripting and style time.
2. Energy: write a Green Metrics Tool usage scenario that loads the DAG view in a headful browser with a fixed set of running tasks and idles on it. Run it for both versions and compare CPU energy. Multiply the per-minute difference by your real screen-hours, not by the benchmark above.

