**Workload:** web (local gateway UI)

#### [Bug] Every refresh adds all rows again
- **Where:** `load()`, called by `setInterval(load, 30000)`
- **Issue:** `load()` appends rows but never removes the old ones. After 30 seconds you have every reading twice, after 5 minutes eleven times. That is why the page gets slower over time.
- **Fix:** replace the old rows instead of appending to them (`tbody.replaceChildren(...)` in the version below).

#### [Security] Sensor data goes into `innerHTML`
- **Where:** `cell()`, `inner.innerHTML = '<span>' + text + '</span>'`
- **Issue:** `sensor_name` and `status` come from the devices. A device name like `<img src=x onerror=...>` runs script in the browser of whoever opens the page.
- **Fix:** set `textContent` instead. The browser then shows the text as text.

#### [High] About 156,000 elements for one page view
- **Where:** the loop in `load()` and `cell()`
- **Issue:** the freeze happens in your browser, not on the gateway. Each reading creates 13 elements (1 row div + 4 × div, div, span). With about 12,000 readings that is about 156,000 elements, and each row goes into the live DOM on its own. Nobody can read 12,000 rows on one screen anyway.
- **Fix:** show 100 rows per page in a plain `<table>`, build them in a `DocumentFragment`, and swap them in with one call. Use `textContent` for the cell values:
  ```html
  <!DOCTYPE html>
  <html>
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>Gateway GW-07 – Readings</title>
    <style>
      table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
      td { padding: 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; font-family: monospace; }
      tr.alarm { background: #fdd; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Sensor readings (last 24 h)</h1>
    <table>
      <tbody id="readings"></tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <button id="prev">Previous</button>
      <span id="page-info"></span>
      <button id="next">Next</button>
    </p>

    <script>
      const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
      const tbody = document.getElementById('readings');
      const info = document.getElementById('page-info');
      let readings = [];
      let page = 0;

      function render() {
        const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(readings.length / PAGE_SIZE));
        page = Math.min(page, pages - 1);
        const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
        for (const r of readings.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE)) {
          const tr = frag.appendChild(document.createElement('tr'));
          if (r.value > r.threshold) tr.className = 'alarm';
          for (const text of [
            new Date(r.ts * 1000).toLocaleString(),
            r.sensor_name,
            r.value.toFixed(2) + ' ' + r.unit,
            r.status,
          ]) {
            tr.appendChild(document.createElement('td')).textContent = text;
          }
        }
        tbody.replaceChildren(frag);
        info.textContent = `Page ${page + 1} of ${pages} (${readings.length} readings)`;
      }

      async function load() {
        const res = await fetch('/api/readings?hours=24');
        readings = await res.json();
        render();
      }

      document.getElementById('prev').onclick = () => { if (page > 0) { page--; render(); } };
      document.getElementById('next').onclick = () => { page++; render(); };

      load();
      setInterval(load, 30000);
    </script>
  </body>
  </html>
  ```
- **Impact:** reduces E and M on every client that opens the page. A page has 100 rows × 5 elements instead of 12,000 × 13. Green Metrics Tool page-load benchmarks tested a 10,000-row table with 14 elements per row, appended one by one. Paginated to 100 rows per page as a plain `<table>` built in a `DocumentFragment`, it used about 56% less CPU energy.

