  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Access log</h1>
  <select id="filter">
    <option value="all">All requests</option>
    <option value="errors">Errors only</option>
  </select>
  <span id="count"></span>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Time</th><th>IP</th><th>Method</th><th>Path</th><th>Status</th><th>ms</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody id="log"></tbody>
  </table>

  <div>
    <button id="prev">Previous</button>
    <span id="page"></span>
    <button id="next">Next</button>
  </div>

  <script>
    const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
    const COLUMNS = ['time', 'ip', 'method', 'path', 'status', 'ms'];
    let entries = [];
    let visible = [];
    let page = 0;

    async function load() {
      const res = await fetch('/api/access-log');
      entries = await res.json();
      applyFilter();
    }

    function applyFilter() {
      const mode = document.getElementById('filter').value;
      visible = mode === 'errors' ? entries.filter(e => e.status >= 400) : entries;
      page = 0;
      render();
    }

    function render() {
      const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(visible.length / PAGE_SIZE));
      const start = page * PAGE_SIZE;
      const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();

      for (const e of visible.slice(start, start + PAGE_SIZE)) {
        const tr = document.createElement('tr');
        if (e.status >= 400) tr.className = 'err';
        for (const key of COLUMNS) {
          const td = document.createElement('td');
          td.textContent = e[key];
          tr.appendChild(td);
        }
        fragment.appendChild(tr);
      }

      document.getElementById('log').replaceChildren(fragment);
      document.getElementById('count').textContent = visible.length + ' lines';
      document.getElementById('page').textContent = 'Page ' + (page + 1) + ' of ' + pages;
      document.getElementById('prev').disabled = page === 0;
      document.getElementById('next').disabled = page >= pages - 1;
    }

    document.getElementById('filter').addEventListener('change', applyFilter);
    document.getElementById('prev').addEventListener('click', () => { page--; render(); });
    document.getElementById('next').addEventListener('click', () => { page++; render(); });
    load();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Green Notes**
- **Smaller DOM with pagination** (reduces E): the page now holds 100 rows instead of all lines, so the browser lays out and paints a few hundred elements, not hundreds of thousands. In Green Metrics Tool browser benchmarks, loading a 10,000-row table built from 14 elements per row used ~1036 J over 30 page loads. The same data paginated to 100 rows per page as a plain `<table>` used ~457 J, about 56% less. That is also why your tab freezes.
- **Fewer elements per row:** the two wrapper elements around every value are gone. A row is now one `<tr>` with six `<td>`. The columns line up as before.
- **One DOM update per page:** rows are collected in a `DocumentFragment` and put in with a single `replaceChildren` call, not one `appendChild` per line on the live page.

The filter still works on the full log, and the line count still shows all matching lines. Browser search (Ctrl+F) now only finds text on the current page. If you often search for a text, add a search box that filters `visible` the same way as the error filter.

