- **Precompressed static text** (reduces E, network): HTML, JS, CSS, SVG and the JSON summaries are compressed once at build time, not on every request. In Green Metrics Tool nginx benchmarks with 2,000 text requests, compressing on every request used about 4× the CPU energy of serving precompressed files. Text sent about 10× fewer bytes with compression. On a local network the byte saving was almost free, so for your home users it matters more than the benchmark shows.
- **Cheap level for dynamic JSON** (reduces E): `/api/` cannot be precompressed, so it uses gzip level 3 and skips tiny responses. In the same benchmark, per-request gzip level 5 cost about 7× the CPU energy of no compression, so keep the level low there.
- **No compression for `.nc` / `.h5`** (reduces E): these formats are already compressed, so gzip would spend CPU and save almost nothing. It would also get in the way of resumed downloads.

