Direction: moving the rotation to CSS is the lower-energy choice. With `requestAnimationFrame`, your JS callback runs on every frame and writes a style each time. With a CSS animation, the browser runs the rotation itself and no script wakes up per frame.

Measured reference: in Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, a 20-second animation in a real browser used ~161 J of CPU energy when driven by `requestAnimationFrame` with style updates, and ~145 J with CSS `@keyframes`. That is about 10% less. This was one specific animation, so don't scale it to your page. A small rotating icon is a much lighter load than that benchmark, so the absolute saving per analyst will be small. Only the fact that CSS is cheaper carries over.

What you can write in the report: "Moving the sync spinner from a JS animation loop to CSS removes per-frame script work. A browser micro-benchmark measured about 10% less CPU energy for CSS keyframes. The saving for our page is small per user and has not been measured yet."

