```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>UI test report</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: sans-serif; margin: 20px; }
    #grid { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; }
    .card { width: 330px; margin: 0; padding: 5px; border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 13px; }
    .card img { display: block; }
    .card.passed { border-left: 4px solid #2a7; }
    .card.failed { border-left: 4px solid #c33; }
    #pager { margin: 12px 0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>UI test report</h1>
  <!-- above the fold: keep it eager -->
  <img src="shots/summary-overview.png" width="800" height="300" alt="Summary">
  <div id="pager">
    <button id="prev">Previous</button>
    <span id="pos"></span>
    <button id="next">Next</button>
  </div>
  <div id="grid"></div>

  <script src="results.js"></script>
  <script>
    // results.js defines: const results = [{name, status, shot, durationMs}, ...]
    const PAGE_SIZE = 50;
    const EAGER_IMAGES = 6; // roughly what fits on the first screen
    const grid = document.getElementById('grid');
    const pos = document.getElementById('pos');
    const prev = document.getElementById('prev');
    const next = document.getElementById('next');
    const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(results.length / PAGE_SIZE));
    let page = 0;

    function render() {
      const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
      results.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE).forEach((r, i) => {
        const card = document.createElement('figure');
        card.className = 'card ' + r.status;

        const img = new Image(320, 200);
        img.src = 'shots/' + r.shot;
        img.alt = r.name;
        if (i >= EAGER_IMAGES) img.loading = 'lazy';

        const cap = document.createElement('figcaption');
        cap.textContent = r.name + ' · ' + r.status + ' · ' + (r.durationMs / 1000).toFixed(1) + ' s';

        card.append(img, cap);
        frag.appendChild(card);
      });
      grid.replaceChildren(frag);
      pos.textContent = 'Page ' + (page + 1) + ' of ' + pages + ' (' + results.length + ' tests)';
      prev.disabled = page === 0;
      next.disabled = page === pages - 1;
    }

    prev.onclick = () => { page--; render(); };
    next.onclick = () => { page++; render(); };
    render();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Green Notes**
- **Smaller DOM** (reduces E on the client): the page shows 50 cards instead of 3000. Each card has 3 elements (`figure`, `img`, `figcaption`) instead of 7, and the cards are added to a `DocumentFragment` and inserted in one step. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, a table with 10,000 rows built from 14 elements per row, appended one by one, used about 56% less CPU energy when paginated to 100 rows per page as a plain table built in a fragment. Your page is a card grid, not a table, so treat that as the direction, not a prediction.
- **Lazy-load screenshots below the first screen** (reduces network and E): the first 6 cards and the summary image stay eager, so nothing visible is delayed. In the same micro-benchmarks, lazy loading saved about 12% on page loads without scrolling and made no difference when the visitor scrolled to the end. With 50 images per page the effect is small. It helps most for people who only look at the top of a page.

Other things to know:
- `results.js` still downloads all 3000 entries. That is plain data and cheap next to the screenshots.
- The status badge is now a colored border and the text in the caption, so the page looks a bit simpler. Everything is set with `textContent`, so test names cannot inject HTML.
- If people mostly want failed tests, a "failed only" filter on `results` before paging would help them more than any page size.

