takes ages on a day of gateway access logs. fix this

```python
import ipaddress
import math
from collections import Counter

INTERNAL_NETS = [
    ipaddress.ip_network("10.0.0.0/8"),
    ipaddress.ip_network("172.16.0.0/12"),
    ipaddress.ip_network("192.168.0.0/16"),
    ipaddress.ip_network("100.64.0.0/10"),
]

OFFICE_NETS = {
    "berlin": ipaddress.ip_network("10.20.0.0/16"),
    "hamburg": ipaddress.ip_network("10.30.0.0/16"),
}


def classify_ip(ip):
    addr = ipaddress.ip_address(ip)
    for name, net in OFFICE_NETS.items():
        if addr in net:
            return "office:" + name
    for net in INTERNAL_NETS:
        if addr in net:
            return "internal"
    if addr.is_loopback:
        return "loopback"
    return "external"


def summarize(path):
    by_class = Counter()
    bytes_by_class = Counter()
    errors_by_class = Counter()
    size_hist = Counter()
    bad_lines = 0
    with open(path) as f:
        for line in f:
            parts = line.split()
            if len(parts) < 10:
                bad_lines += 1
                continue
            ip, status, size = parts[0], parts[8], parts[9]
            cls = classify_ip(ip)
            by_class[cls] += 1
            if status.startswith("5"):
                errors_by_class[cls] += 1
            if size != "-":
                n = int(size)
                bytes_by_class[cls] += n
                if n > 0:
                    size_hist[int(math.log10(n))] += 1
    return by_class, bytes_by_class, errors_by_class, size_hist, bad_lines
```

The DOM is the tree of objects the browser builds from your HTML. Every `<tr>`, `<td>`, `<div>` and `<span>` becomes one object that the browser must store, style and lay out. Small text data can still make a huge tree: 10,000 readings with 14 elements each is 140,000 objects.

It matters for energy too, because that work runs as CPU time on the computer of whoever opens the page. The gateway only sends the data. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, 30 page loads of a table with 10,000 rows (14 elements per row, wrappers around every cell, rows appended to the page one by one) used ~1036 J. The same data paginated to 100 rows per page, as a plain `<table>` built in a `DocumentFragment`, used ~457 J. That is about 56% less.

The fix has three parts:
- **Show one page of rows at a time**, not all rows.
- **Use fewer elements per row**: a plain `<td>` with text, no extra wrappers.
- **Build the rows off-screen** in a `DocumentFragment` and add them once.

```javascript
function renderPage(tbody, readings, page, pageSize = 100) {
  const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
  const start = page * pageSize;
  for (const r of readings.slice(start, start + pageSize)) {
    const tr = document.createElement('tr');
    for (const value of [r.time, r.sensor, r.value]) {
      const td = document.createElement('td');
      td.textContent = value;
      tr.appendChild(td);
    }
    frag.appendChild(tr);
  }
  tbody.replaceChildren(frag);
}
```

When it does not matter: a table with a few dozen rows is fine as it is. This is for lists with thousands of rows. If people mostly look for one sensor or one time range, a filter on the gateway side (send only the matching rows) is simpler than paging through everything.

