```

**Green Notes**
- **`dict.get(key, default)` instead of `try/except KeyError`** (reduces E): your clients mostly omit these parameters, so most requests hit the "missing key" path, and raising and catching an exception is the slow part. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, 10,000,000 dict lookups with about half of the keys missing used about 12% less CPU energy with `dict.get` than with `try/except KeyError`. Here the miss rate is likely higher than that, but I have no measurement for your traffic, so profile it before you promise a number.
- **Unknown `sort` values:** the old code also fell back to `created_at` through `KeyError`. One `SORT_FIELDS.get(params.get("sort"), "created_at")` covers both cases with the same result.
- **`int()` keeps its `try/except ValueError`:** bad numbers are rare, and a `try` block that does not raise costs almost nothing. The cost is in raising, so there is no reason to change it. I moved it into `_int_param` to remove the duplicated blocks. The error messages are the same.

The results and `BadRequest` messages are identical for every input the old code handled.

**Workload:** web (static report generated by a log pipeline)

#### [Security] Log content injected as HTML
- **Where:** `row.innerHTML = ... + e.message + ...` in `render()`
- **Issue:** error messages carry attacker-controlled text such as request paths and user agents. A logged `<img src=x onerror=...>` runs in on-call's browser. `e.level` inside the `class` attribute has the same problem.
- **Fix:** set every value with `textContent`. The table rewrite below does this.

#### [High] Up to 20k entries rendered at once, rebuilt on every keystroke
- **Where:** `render()` and the `input` listener
- **Issue:** each entry is 11 elements, and every row is appended to the live DOM one by one. With 20k entries that is over 200,000 elements. Each keystroke in the filter throws them away and builds them again.
- **Fix:** use a plain table, render 100 rows per page into a `DocumentFragment`, and filter in memory:
  ```html
  <input id="filter" placeholder="Filter by service">
  <table id="errors" class="log-list">
    <thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Level</th><th>Host</th><th>Service</th><th>Message</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody></tbody>
  </table>
  <p><button id="prev">Prev</button> <span id="page"></span> <button id="next">Next</button></p>
  ```
  ```javascript
  const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
  const tbody = document.querySelector('#errors tbody');
  const filterEl = document.getElementById('filter');
  let all = [], shown = [], page = 0;

  fetch('errors.json')
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(rows => { all = rows; applyFilter(); });

  function applyFilter() {
    const q = filterEl.value;
    shown = q ? all.filter(e => e.service.includes(q)) : all;
    page = 0;
    render();
  }

  function render() {
    const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
    for (const e of shown.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE)) {
      const tr = document.createElement('tr');
      tr.className = e.level;
      for (const v of [e.ts, e.level, e.host, e.service, e.message]) {
        const td = document.createElement('td');
        td.textContent = v;
        tr.appendChild(td);
      }
      frag.appendChild(tr);
    }
    tbody.replaceChildren(frag);
    const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(shown.length / PAGE_SIZE));
    document.getElementById('page').textContent = `${page + 1} / ${pages} (${shown.length} errors)`;
  }

  document.getElementById('prev').onclick = () => { if (page > 0) { page--; render(); } };
  document.getElementById('next').onclick = () => { if ((page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE < shown.length) { page++; render(); } };
  filterEl.addEventListener('input', applyFilter);
  ```
- **Impact:** E on the client, on every page open and every filter keystroke. In Green Metrics Tool page-load benchmarks, a 10,000-row table with 14 elements per row, appended one by one, used about 56% less CPU energy when it was paginated to 100 rows per page as a plain `<table>` built in a `DocumentFragment`.
- **Note:** browser Ctrl+F now only searches the current page, so tell on-call to use the filter. In `report.css`, move the `.log-row` / `.cell` / `.badge.<level>` styles to `tr` / `td` / `tr.<level>`.

#### [Medium] Charts shipped only as large PNGs
- **Where:** `<section class="charts">`
- **Issue:** three PNGs at ~1.5 MB each are the heaviest download on a phone. AVIF or WebP versions are usually much smaller.
- **Fix:** encode the extra formats once in the nightly job, right after matplotlib writes the PNGs (e.g. `avifenc`, `cwebp`). Never encode per request. Keep the PNG as the fallback:
  ```html
  <picture>
    <source srcset="charts/ingest-lag-heatmap.avif" type="image/avif">
    <source srcset="charts/ingest-lag-heatmap.webp" type="image/webp">
    <img src="charts/ingest-lag-heatmap.png" alt="Ingest lag per host and hour">
  </picture>
  ```
  Do the same for the other two charts.
- **Impact:** mostly network, fewer bytes per page open. Client CPU does not go up. In Green Metrics Tool page-load benchmarks, PNG was the most expensive format to decode (~181 J for 50 page loads, AVIF ~159 J, WebP ~150 J). AVIF encoding is costly, about 7× JPG in the same benchmarks, but that is fine for a few charts encoded once a night. Check the heatmap at your quality setting. If lossy artifacts blur the color steps, use lossless WebP for that chart.

You can do this with CSS alone. Your existing JS only has to set `edge--active`, and nothing has to run on a timer.

```css
.pipeline {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
}

.stage {
  padding: 4px 12px;
  border: 2px solid #8c959f;
  border-radius: 6px;
  font: 600 13px/1.4 system-ui, sans-serif;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.stage--done    { border-color: #2da44e; }
.stage--running { border-color: #d29922; }
.stage--failed  { border-color: #cf222e; color: #cf222e; }
.stage--pending { border-style: dashed; color: #57606a; }

.edge {
  position: relative;
  flex: 1 1 48px;
  min-width: 32px;
  height: 4px;
  overflow: hidden;              /* clips the moving dash layer */
  background: #d0d7de;
}
.edge--done { background: #2da44e; }

/* Only the edge into the running stage moves */
.edge--active { background: transparent; }
.edge--active::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  /* one dash period (16px) wider on the left, so the loop is seamless */
  inset: 0 0 0 -16px;
  background: repeating-linear-gradient(
    90deg,
    #d29922 0 8px,
    transparent 8px 16px
  );
  animation: edge-flow 0.8s linear infinite;
}

@keyframes edge-flow {
  to { transform: translateX(16px); }  /* exactly one period */
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .edge--active::before { animation: none; }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **CSS keyframes instead of a JS timer** (reduces E): the browser runs the animation by itself, so no script runs every 30 ms on each open dashboard. Green Metrics Tool measured a 20-second animation in a real browser. CSS `@keyframes` used ~145 J of CPU energy and a JavaScript `requestAnimationFrame` loop with style updates used ~161 J, so CSS used about 10% less. That test compared against `requestAnimationFrame`, not `setInterval`.
- **Move it with `transform`, not `background-position`** (reduces E): the dash layer slides as one piece, so the browser doesn't have to repaint the gradient every frame. That is why the pseudo-element is one period wider and `overflow: hidden` clips it.
- **Animate only the active edge** (reduces E): done and pending edges stay static. When a stage finishes, your JS swaps the class and the animation stops. A dashboard left open with no running pipelines does no animation work at all.

