    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }

    location /assets/ {
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
    }

    # tokenizer.json + model.onnx for in-browser inference
    location /models {
        alias /srv/models/onnx/;
    }

    location /v1/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_read_timeout 120s;
    }
}
```

**Workload:** mixed (web frontend + AI/ML inference API)

#### [Security] Path traversal through `alias`
- **Where:** `location /models { alias /srv/models/onnx/; }`
- **Issue:** the location has no trailing slash, but the alias does. A request for `/models../secrets.env` maps to `/srv/models/onnx/../secrets.env`, so clients can read files in `/srv/models/` outside the `onnx` directory.
- **Fix:** give both the same trailing slash: `location /models/ { alias /srv/models/onnx/; }`.

#### [High] Static files are gzipped at max level on every request
- **Where:** `gzip on` + `gzip_comp_level 9`, applied to the playground bundle and `tokenizer.json`
- **Issue:** the JS, CSS, HTML and tokenizer files only change on deploy, but nginx compresses them again for every download. It does this at the most expensive level. The same level also applies to every proxied `/v1/` JSON response.
- **Fix:** precompress static files once at build time and turn on `gzip_static`. Keep on-the-fly gzip for the dynamic API responses, but at a moderate level.
- **Impact:** reduces E on the server for every page load and model download. In Green Metrics Tool benchmarks with nginx serving text assets, gzip level 5 on every request cost about 4× the CPU energy of serving precompressed files. Both sent about 10× fewer bytes than no compression, so keep compression on. Just do the work once instead of once per request.

#### [Medium] Compressing images and model weights
- **Where:** `gzip_types ... image/png image/jpeg application/octet-stream`
- **Issue:** PNG and JPEG are already compressed, so gzip burns CPU and saves nothing. `model.onnx` gets the default type `application/octet-stream`. It is the largest file you serve, and it gets compressed at level 9 on every download. Float weights also shrink much less than text does.
- **Fix:** remove these types from `gzip_types`. If a compressed `model.onnx` is worth having, create `model.onnx.gz` once at build time and `gzip_static` will serve it.

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name infer.internal.example.com;

    root /srv/playground/dist;
    index index.html;

    gzip on;
    gzip_comp_level 5;          # dynamic API responses only
    gzip_proxied any;
    gzip_vary on;
    gzip_types text/css application/javascript application/json;
    gzip_static on;             # serve prebuilt *.gz files when present

    client_max_body_size 20m;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }

    location /assets/ {
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
    }

    location /models/ {
        alias /srv/models/onnx/;
    }

    location /v1/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_read_timeout 120s;
    }
}
```

Add this to the deploy step. A high level is fine here because it runs once per build:

```bash
find /srv/playground/dist /srv/models/onnx -type f \
  \( -name '*.html' -o -name '*.js' -o -name '*.css' -o -name '*.json' \) \
  -exec gzip -k -9 -f {} +
```

Run it on every deploy. Otherwise nginx keeps serving an old `.gz` next to a newer file. `gzip_static` needs `ngx_http_gzip_static_module`. Most distro packages and the official image include it (`nginx -V` lists it).

**Already good:** long cache lifetime for `/assets/`, and `gzip_proxied any` keeps API JSON compressed.

