**Workload:** web (e-commerce category page)

#### [Security] Product data is written into the page as HTML
- **Where:** `card.innerHTML = ...` in `loadCatalog`
- **Issue:** `p.name`, `p.image` and `p.priceFormatted` go straight into markup. If any of them contains a quote or a tag (for example from a supplier feed), it breaks the card or runs script in your shop.
- **Fix:** build the cards with `textContent` and property assignment, as in the next finding.

#### [High] The whole category is in the DOM at once
- **Where:** `products.forEach(...)` appending every product to `.grid`
- **Issue:** 6,000 cards with about 10 elements each put around 60,000 elements on the page, appended one by one to the live DOM. Every style recalculation, layout and scroll has to deal with all of them, even though a visitor sees a dozen at a time. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, 30 page loads of a table with 10,000 rows built from 14 elements per row, appended to the live DOM, used ~1036 J. The same data paginated to 100 rows per page, as plain elements built in a `DocumentFragment`, used ~457 J, about 56% less. That was a table, not a product grid, but the cause is the same.
- **Fix:** keep the array in memory, render one page of 100 products, and drop the wrapper elements. The category filter keeps working, because it still loads the array:
  ```javascript
  const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
  let products = [];

  function renderPage(page) {
    const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
    for (const p of products.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE)) {
      const card = document.createElement('article');
      card.className = 'card';

      const img = document.createElement('img');
      img.src = p.image;
      img.alt = p.name;
      card.append(img);

      if (p.onSale) {
        const badge = document.createElement('span');
        badge.className = 'badge-sale';
        badge.textContent = 'Sale';
        card.append(badge);
      }

      const title = document.createElement('h3');
      title.className = 'card-title';
      title.textContent = p.name;

      const price = document.createElement('span');
      price.className = 'card-price';
      price.textContent = p.priceFormatted;

      card.append(title, price);
      frag.append(card);
    }
    grid.replaceChildren(frag);
  }

  async function loadCatalog(category) {
    const res = await fetch('/api/products?category=' + encodeURIComponent(category));
    products = await res.json();
    renderPage(0);
  }
  ```
  Add Previous / Next buttons that call `renderPage`. A "Load more" button would grow the DOM again. The CSS gets shorter, because the wrapper classes go away. These rules keep the card looking the same (the badge moves to `20px` because it is now positioned against the card, 12px padding plus the old 8px):
  ```css
  .card { position: relative; padding: 12px; background: #fff; border: 1px solid #e5e5e5; border-radius: 8px; }
  .card img { display: block; width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 1 / 1; object-fit: cover; }
  .card-title { font-size: 14px; margin: 8px 0 0; min-height: 2.6em; }
  .card-price { display: block; font-weight: 600; }
  .badge-sale { top: 20px; left: 20px; }
  ```
- **Impact:** E on the client for every visit, plus memory (M). It grows with the size of the category. Visitors now see 100 products per page instead of one endless list, so check this with the shop owner.

#### [Medium] Badge pulse is driven by JavaScript on every frame
- **Where:** `pulseBadges` with `requestAnimationFrame`
- **Issue:** the script runs on every frame, queries the whole DOM for badges and writes an inline style to each one, for as long as the page is visible. A CSS animation on `transform` does the same job in the browser's own animation pipeline without running your script. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks of a 20-second animation, `requestAnimationFrame` with style updates used ~161 J of CPU energy and CSS `@keyframes` ~145 J, about 10% less. The gain is moderate and grows with the number of animated elements and the time the page stays open.
- **Fix:** delete `pulseBadges` and its `requestAnimationFrame` call, and put the same motion in the stylesheet (a period of about 1.9 s matches your `t / 300`):
  ```css
  .badge-sale { animation: badge-pulse 1.9s ease-in-out infinite; }

  @keyframes badge-pulse {
    0%, 100% { transform: scale(1); }
    50%      { transform: scale(1.08); }
  }
  ```
- **Impact:** E on the client. With 100 cards per page there are also far fewer badges to animate.

**Already good:** a fixed `aspect-ratio` on the image avoids layout shifts while images load, the animation only touches `transform` (no layout), and the category is URL-encoded in the request.

