**Workload:** web (e-commerce frontend)

#### [Bug] Failed request throws away the whole order
- **Where:** `#add-all` click handler
- **Issue:** the handler never checks the response. If `/api/cart/bulk` fails, the page still redirects to `/cart`, and the buyer loses every quantity they typed.
- **Fix:**
  ```javascript
  document.querySelector("#add-all").addEventListener("click", async () => {
    const lines = [];
    tbody.querySelectorAll(".qty").forEach((input) => {
      if (parseInt(input.value, 10) > 0) lines.push({ sku: input.dataset.sku, qty: input.value });
    });
    const res = await fetch("/api/cart/bulk", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      body: JSON.stringify({ lines }),
    });
    if (!res.ok) {
      alert("Could not add the items to your cart. Please try again.");
      return;
    }
    location.href = "/cart";
  });
  ```

#### [Bug] Zero and negative quantities are sent
- **Where:** `if (input.value)` and `updateTotal`
- **Issue:** `"0"` and `"-3"` are truthy strings. `min="0"` only limits the spinner, so a typed `-3` gets through. These lines go to the cart endpoint, and negative values also lower the total.
- **Fix:** only accept `parseInt(value, 10) > 0`. The snippets above and below do this.

#### [Security] Catalog fields injected with `innerHTML`
- **Where:** `tr.innerHTML = ...` with `p.name`, `p.sku`, `p.unit`
- **Issue:** product names may come from supplier feeds or imports. A name with markup then runs as HTML/JS in a logged-in buyer session that can place orders.
- **Fix:** set cells with `textContent` (see below).

#### [High] ~119,000 DOM nodes appended one row at a time
- **Where:** `loadCatalog` loop
- **Issue:** each row has 17 elements (`tr`, 5 `td`, 6 wrapper `div`s, 4 `span`s, the input). With 7,000 SKUs that is about 119,000 nodes, and each row goes straight into the live `tbody`. The code then attaches 7,000 listeners, and each keystroke queries all 7,000 inputs again. This causes the freeze.
- **Fix:** keep quantities in a `Map` so they survive paging. Render 100 plain rows per page from a `DocumentFragment`, and add a search box so buyers still find SKUs fast. Use one delegated listener.
  ```javascript
  const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
  const tbody = document.querySelector("#quick-order tbody");
  const totalEl = document.querySelector("#order-total");
  const pageEl = document.querySelector("#qo-page");
  const qty = new Map(); // sku -> quantity, kept across pages and searches
  let products = [];
  let bySku = new Map();
  let filtered = [];
  let page = 0;

  function td(text) {
    const el = document.createElement("td");
    el.textContent = text;
    return el;
  }

  function render() {
    const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
    for (const p of filtered.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE)) {
      const input = document.createElement("input");
      input.type = "number";
      input.min = "0";
      input.dataset.sku = p.sku;
      input.value = qty.get(p.sku) ?? "";
      const qtyCell = document.createElement("td");
      qtyCell.append(input);
      const tr = document.createElement("tr");
      tr.append(td(p.sku), td(p.name), td(p.unit), td((p.priceCents / 100).toFixed(2)), qtyCell);
      frag.append(tr);
    }
    tbody.replaceChildren(frag);
    pageEl.textContent = `${page + 1} / ${Math.max(1, Math.ceil(filtered.length / PAGE_SIZE))}`;
  }

  async function loadCatalog() {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/b2b/catalog?customer=${window.CUSTOMER_ID}`);
    products = await res.json();
    bySku = new Map(products.map((p) => [p.sku, p]));
    filtered = products;
    render();
  }

  tbody.addEventListener("input", (e) => {
    const sku = e.target.dataset.sku;
    if (!sku) return;
    const n = parseInt(e.target.value, 10);
    if (n > 0) qty.set(sku, n);
    else qty.delete(sku);
    let cents = 0;
