```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Print the total size of a directory tree."""
import os
import sys
from os.path import *


def human(n):
    for unit in ('B', 'KB', 'MB', 'GB'):
        if n < 1024:
            return f'{n:.1f} {unit}'
        n /= 1024
    return f'{n:.1f} TB'


def main(argv):
    root = argv[1] if len(argv) > 1 else '.'
    if not isdir(root):
        print(f'not a directory: {root}', file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    total = 0
    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(root):
        for name in files:
            path = join(dirpath, name)
            if islink(path):
                continue
            total += getsize(path)
    print(human(total))
    return 0


if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
```

The slow part was most likely the 20,000 rows in the page at once. The browser has to build and lay out every one of them. This version loads the file once, but only puts 100 rows in the page at a time.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Simulation results</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; margin: 1rem 2rem; }
    table { border-collapse: collapse; }
    th, td { padding: 0.25rem 0.75rem; text-align: right; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; }
    nav { margin: 0.75rem 0; display: flex; gap: 0.75rem; align-items: center; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Simulation results</h1>
  <p id="status">Loading…</p>

  <nav aria-label="Pages">
    <button type="button" id="prev">Previous</button>
    <span id="page-info"></span>
    <button type="button" id="next">Next</button>
  </nav>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Step</th><th>Time</th><th>Energy</th><th>Temperature</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody id="rows"></tbody>
  </table>

  <script>
    const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
    const FIELDS = ["step", "time", "energy", "temperature"];

    const tbody = document.getElementById("rows");
    const status = document.getElementById("status");
    const info = document.getElementById("page-info");
    const prev = document.getElementById("prev");
    const next = document.getElementById("next");

    let data = [];
    let page = 0;

    function render() {
      const start = page * PAGE_SIZE;
      const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();

      for (const item of data.slice(start, start + PAGE_SIZE)) {
        const tr = document.createElement("tr");
        for (const field of FIELDS) {
          const td = document.createElement("td");
          td.textContent = item[field];
          tr.appendChild(td);
        }
        fragment.appendChild(tr);
      }

      tbody.replaceChildren(fragment);

      const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(data.length / PAGE_SIZE));
      info.textContent = `Page ${page + 1} of ${pages}`;
      prev.disabled = page === 0;
      next.disabled = page >= pages - 1;
    }

    prev.addEventListener("click", () => { page -= 1; render(); });
    next.addEventListener("click", () => { page += 1; render(); });

    fetch("results.json")
      .then((response) => {
        if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
        return response.json();
      })
      .then((json) => {
        data = json;
        status.textContent = `${data.length} steps`;
        render();
      })
      .catch((error) => {
        status.textContent = `Could not load results.json: ${error.message}`;
      });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open it through a small web server (for example `python -m http.server` in the folder), because `fetch` does not read local files from `file://`.

**Green Notes**
- **Small DOM** (reduces E on the client): the page holds 100 rows instead of 20,000, so the browser builds and lays out far fewer elements. In Green Metrics Tool browser benchmarks, loading a table of 10,000 rows with wrapper elements around every cell used ~1036 J. The same data paginated to 100 rows per page as a plain `<table>` used ~457 J, about 56% less.
- **Plain table, one batch** (reduces E): each row is just `<tr>` and `<td>`, with no extra wrapper elements. Rows are built in a `DocumentFragment` and swapped in with one `replaceChildren` call, so the live page is updated once per page, not once per row.
- **`textContent`** instead of building HTML strings: it is faster and also safe if a value is not a number.

Browser search (Ctrl+F) now finds text only on the current page. If you need to search all rows, add a small filter box that filters `data` before it renders.

