Your colleague is right in direction, but the size of the saving is small and I can't give you a number for your page.

**Measured reference:** in Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, a 20-second animation in a real browser used ~161 J of CPU energy when driven by JavaScript `requestAnimationFrame` with style updates, and ~145 J with CSS `@keyframes`. That is about 10% less for CSS.

Your spinner has the same shape as that test: a `requestAnimationFrame` loop that writes a `transform` on every frame. The CSS version removes the script work on every frame, and the browser can run the rotation on its own.

```css
.spinner {
  width: 16px;
  height: 16px;
  border: 2px solid #ccc;
  border-top-color: #2b7be4;
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: spin 1s linear infinite;
}

@keyframes spin {
  to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}
```

Then delete the whole `<script>` block. Two small behavior notes:
- Your loop turns 6° per frame, so the speed depends on the screen refresh rate. The CSS version turns once per second on every screen.
- Your loop keeps running even when no build is running. With CSS, the animation only exists while a `.spinner` element is on the page.

A 10% gain on one small spinner is tiny. The benchmark ran one animation for 20 seconds, so I would not scale it to your page. The CSS version is also shorter and easier to maintain, which is a good reason on its own.

**How to measure it:**
1. Open the page with 5-10 running builds, once with the old code and once with the new.
2. Compare CPU use for the same time span, for example 60 seconds, in the browser's task manager or the performance panel.
3. For energy, run both pages as a usage scenario in the Green Metrics Tool, or at least compare CPU time with `perf stat` as a proxy.

