Everything else stays the same. Partners still get compressed JSON, and the cluster pipeline doesn't change.

The trade-off is that every download of a big summary is compressed again. If downloads grow later, the `find ... gzip -kf` line can run on the VM after each rsync, for example from a systemd path unit, without touching the cluster side.

On the LAN, I'd turn it off.

Compression pays off by saving bytes on slow or metered links. On a fast local network those bytes cost almost nothing, so you mostly pay CPU on both ends: nginx compresses, the browser decompresses. In Green Metrics Tool nginx benchmarks on a local network, 2,000 requests for text assets used ~7.3 J with no compression, ~14 J with precompressed files and ~54 J with gzip per request. Per-request gzip cost about 7× no compression, and even precompressed files cost about 2×, because of the decompression on the client.

Ubuntu's default `nginx.conf` sets `gzip on;` in the `http` block, so disable it explicitly:

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name oceanportal.example.org;

    root /srv/oceanportal/site;
    index index.html;

    gzip off;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $http_x_forwarded_proto;
    }
}
```

