I filled in `renderDeliveries`. It builds a plain table and shows 100 rows per page with Prev/Next buttons. `loadDeliveries` stays as it is.

```javascript
const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
const COLUMNS = ['id', 'endpoint', 'status_code', 'attempts', 'last_attempt_at'];

function renderDeliveries(items) {
  const root = document.getElementById('deliveries');
  const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(items.length / PAGE_SIZE));
  let page = 0;

  const table = document.createElement('table');
  const headRow = table.createTHead().insertRow();
  for (const name of COLUMNS) {
    const th = document.createElement('th');
    th.textContent = name;
    headRow.appendChild(th);
  }
  const tbody = table.createTBody();

  const prev = document.createElement('button');
  prev.textContent = 'Prev';
  const next = document.createElement('button');
  next.textContent = 'Next';
  const label = document.createElement('span');

  function draw() {
    const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
    const start = page * PAGE_SIZE;
    for (const item of items.slice(start, start + PAGE_SIZE)) {
      const tr = document.createElement('tr');
      for (const col of COLUMNS) {
        const td = document.createElement('td');
        td.textContent = item[col] ?? '';
        tr.appendChild(td);
      }
      frag.appendChild(tr);
    }
    tbody.replaceChildren(frag);
    label.textContent = ` Page ${page + 1} of ${pages} (${items.length} deliveries) `;
    prev.disabled = page === 0;
    next.disabled = page >= pages - 1;
  }

  prev.addEventListener('click', () => { page -= 1; draw(); });
  next.addEventListener('click', () => { page += 1; draw(); });

  const nav = document.createElement('div');
  nav.append(prev, label, next);

  root.replaceChildren(nav, table);
  draw();
}
```

I used `textContent` for every cell. Endpoint URLs and other fields come from outside, so they are never parsed as HTML.

**Green Notes**
- **Paginate large tables** (reduces E on the client): the browser only holds about 100 rows instead of 20k+. In Green Metrics Tool browser benchmarks, a 10,000-row table paginated to 100 rows per page used about 56% less CPU energy over 30 page loads than the same data rendered all at once.
- **Plain `<table>` built in a `DocumentFragment`** (reduces E): one plain `<tr>`/`<td>` per row, with no wrapper elements. The rows are inserted into the live DOM once per page, not one by one. This was part of the same benchmark.
- **Page change only swaps the rows** (reduces E): `replaceChildren` replaces the visible rows in one step, and the table and header stay in place.

