  transition: transform 100ms linear;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .meter span { transition: none; }
}
```

Remove the `.is-listening` keyframe rules and the `@keyframes bounce` block. The container and the span styles stay as they were.

```javascript
export function setLevels(el, levels) {
  const bars = el.children;
  for (let i = 0; i < bars.length; i++) {
    const level = Math.min(1, Math.max(0.05, levels[i] ?? 0));
    bars[i].style.setProperty('--level', level);
  }
}

export function stopMeter(el) {
  setLevels(el, []);
}
```

Call `setLevels(meterEl, levels)` in the worker `message` handler. The transition is 100 ms, the same as your update interval, so the bars move smoothly without any extra code.

**Green Notes**
- **Transition instead of an rAF interpolation loop** (reduces E): your JS now runs only when the worker sends data, not on every frame. The browser does the in-between frames. Only `transform` changes, so layout is not recalculated.
- The clamp keeps a bad level value from stretching a bar outside the 32px box. `stopMeter` drops all bars back to the low resting height.

Test report page from our CI dashboard. Runs of the big monorepo have ~15k test cases, and when a build goes red the tab takes ages to become usable. Every dev opens this on every failed pipeline. Review it, diffs only please.

```javascript
// ci-dashboard/static/test-report.js
const runId = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('run');
let allTests = [];

async function loadResults() {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/runs/${encodeURIComponent(runId)}/tests`);
  allTests = await res.json(); // [{ suite, name, status, durationMs, message }]
  renderSummary(allTests);
  renderTests(allTests);
}

function renderSummary(tests) {
  const failed = tests.filter((t) => t.status === 'failed').length;
  const skipped = tests.filter((t) => t.status === 'skipped').length;
  document.getElementById('summary').textContent =
    `${tests.length} tests, ${failed} failed, ${skipped} skipped`;
}

function renderTests(tests) {
  const list = document.getElementById('tests');
  list.innerHTML = '';
  for (const t of tests) {
    const row = document.createElement('div');
    row.className = `test-row ${t.status}`;
    row.innerHTML = `
      <div class="cell suite"><span class="label">${t.suite}</span></div>
      <div class="cell name"><span class="label">${t.name}</span></div>
      <div class="cell status"><span class="badge">${t.status}</span></div>
      <div class="cell duration"><span class="label">${(t.durationMs / 1000).toFixed(2)}s</span></div>
      <div class="cell message"><pre>${t.message ?? ''}</pre></div>`;
    list.appendChild(row);
  }
}

document.getElementById('only-failed').addEventListener('change', (e) => {
  renderTests(e.target.checked ? allTests.filter((t) => t.status === 'failed') : allTests);
});

loadResults();
```

**Workload:** web (internal CI dashboard, opened on every failed pipeline)

#### [Security] Test output injected as HTML
- **Where:** `renderTests`, the `row.innerHTML` template
- **Issue:** `message`, `name` and `suite` come from test output. A failing test that prints `<img src=x onerror=...>` runs script in the dashboard, with the session of whoever opens the report.
- **Fix:** set `textContent` on the cells. The High fix below already does this.

#### [Bug] HTTP errors leave a blank page
- **Where:** `loadResults`
- **Issue:** on a 404 or 500, `res.json()` throws (or parses an error body). The page stays empty and the error only shows in the console.
- **Fix:**
  ```javascript
  async function loadResults() {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/runs/${encodeURIComponent(runId)}/tests`);
    if (!res.ok) {
      document.getElementById('summary').textContent = `Could not load results (HTTP ${res.status})`;
      return;
    }
    allTests = await res.json();
    renderSummary(allTests);
    renderTests(allTests);
  }
  ```

#### [High] Every test case rendered at once, wrapped and appended one by one
- **Where:** `renderTests`, and the `only-failed` handler, which re-renders everything again
- **Issue:** each row is 11 elements (row div, cell divs, spans, `pre`), and each row goes into the live DOM separately. On a large run that means a huge DOM to parse, lay out and keep in memory, and you pay for it on every load and every toggle.
- **Fix:** a plain table, 100 rows per page, built in a `DocumentFragment`:
  ```html
  <table id="tests">
    <thead><tr><th>Suite</th><th>Test</th><th>Status</th><th>Duration</th><th>Message</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody></tbody>
  </table>
  <nav><button id="prev">Prev</button> <span id="page-info"></span> <button id="next">Next</button></nav>
  ```
  ```javascript
  const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
  let shown = [];
  let page = 0;

  function renderTests(tests) {
    shown = tests;
    page = 0;
    renderPage();
  }

  function renderPage() {
    const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
    for (const t of shown.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE)) {
      const tr = document.createElement('tr');
      tr.className = t.status;
      for (const text of [t.suite, t.name, t.status, `${(t.durationMs / 1000).toFixed(2)}s`, t.message ?? '']) {
        const td = document.createElement('td');
        td.textContent = text;
        tr.appendChild(td);
      }
      frag.appendChild(tr);
    }
    document.querySelector('#tests tbody').replaceChildren(frag);
    const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(shown.length / PAGE_SIZE));
    document.getElementById('page-info').textContent = `Page ${page + 1} of ${pages}`;
    document.getElementById('prev').disabled = page === 0;
    document.getElementById('next').disabled = page >= pages - 1;
  }

  document.getElementById('prev').addEventListener('click', () => { page--; renderPage(); });
  document.getElementById('next').addEventListener('click', () => { page++; renderPage(); });
  ```
  Move the `.test-row` / `.cell` styles to `tr` / `td`, and add `#tests td:last-child { white-space: pre-wrap; }` so stack traces keep their line breaks. Behavior change: the browser's Ctrl+F only searches the current page now. Toggling `only-failed` goes back to page 1.
- **Impact:** E and M on every client, every time a failed run is opened. In Green Metrics Tool page-load benchmarks, a 10,000-row table with wrappers around every cell, appended row by row, used about 56% less CPU energy when paginated to 100 rows per page as a plain table built in a `DocumentFragment`.

**Already good:** `runId` is URL-encoded. The summary uses `textContent`. The filter works on data already in memory instead of fetching it again.

