**Workload:** web (client-side rendering of a large result set)

#### [Security] Filenames and labels go into `innerHTML`
- **Where:** the `tr.innerHTML = ...` string in the `forEach`
- **Issue:** `item.file` is a filename from the batch input. A file named like `<img src=x onerror=...>` would run as HTML in the page. Building HTML from strings also makes the browser parse 10000 small fragments.
- **Fix:** create the cells with `createElement` and set `textContent`. This is shown in the code below.

#### [High] 10000 rows in the DOM, each with about 10 elements
- **Where:** the `forEach` that appends every item to `#rows`
- **Issue:** each row has a `<tr>`, three `<td>`, plus a `<div>` and a `<span>` inside every cell. That is about 100000 elements for the browser to build, style and lay out, and rows are added to the live table one at a time. This is most likely why the page is slow and the fan spins up.
- **Fix:** show 100 rows per page, drop the `div` and `span` wrappers (put the class on the `<td>`), and build the rows in a `DocumentFragment` before inserting them once.
- **Impact:** reduces E on the client. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, 30 page loads of a 10,000-row table with wrappers around every cell used about 56% less CPU energy when the same data was paginated to 100 rows per page as a plain table built in a `DocumentFragment`.

#### [Low] Unused CSS rules
- **Where:** `.navbar`, `.card`, `.modal`, `.btn`, `.btn-primary`, `.tooltip`, `.sidebar`, `.footer`, `.cell-inner`
- **Issue:** nothing in the page uses these rules. They look left over from a template.
- **Fix:** delete them. For a stylesheet this small this saves a few bytes, not measurable CPU. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, removing unused CSS from a 16 KB stylesheet changed client energy by about 2%, which is within noise. It is only worth it because it also makes the file easier to read.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Batch results</title>
<style>
  body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 20px; }
  .results-table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
  th, td { padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; text-align: left; }
  .label { font-weight: bold; }
  .conf-high { color: #0a7a2f; }
  .conf-low { color: #b00020; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<h1>Batch results</h1>
<div id="summary"></div>
<table class="results-table">
  <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Label</th><th>Confidence</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody id="rows"></tbody>
</table>
<p>
  <button id="prev">Previous</button>
  <span id="page"></span>
  <button id="next">Next</button>
</p>
<script>
const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
let items = [];
let page = 0;

function cell(text, className) {
  const td = document.createElement('td');
  if (className) td.className = className;
  td.textContent = text;
  return td;
}

function render() {
  const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(items.length / PAGE_SIZE));
  const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
  for (const item of items.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE)) {
    const tr = document.createElement('tr');
    tr.append(
      cell(item.file),
      cell(item.label, 'label'),
      cell((item.conf * 100).toFixed(1) + '%', item.conf >= 0.8 ? 'conf-high' : 'conf-low')
    );
    frag.append(tr);
  }
  document.getElementById('rows').replaceChildren(frag);
  document.getElementById('page').textContent = 'Page ' + (page + 1) + ' of ' + pages;
  document.getElementById('prev').disabled = page === 0;
  document.getElementById('next').disabled = page >= pages - 1;
}

document.getElementById('prev').onclick = () => { page--; render(); };
document.getElementById('next').onclick = () => { page++; render(); };

fetch('/api/batch/latest')
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(data => {
    items = data;
    document.getElementById('summary').textContent = items.length + ' images';
    render();
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

The row order is unchanged. The browser's "find in page" now only searches the 100 rows on screen. If people need to search all results, add a filter box that narrows `items` before `render()`.

**Green Notes**
- **Smaller DOM** (reduces E): 100 plain rows per page instead of 10000 rows with wrappers.
- **DocumentFragment** (reduces E): one insert per page instead of one per row.
- **Unused CSS** (saves network bytes only): removed for clarity, with no measurable CPU effect at this size.

