```javascript
#!/usr/bin/env node
// scripts/build-ui.mjs - bundles the web UI for `pipewatch serve`
import { readdir, readFile, writeFile, mkdir, copyFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import path from 'node:path';

const SRC = 'src/ui';
const OUT = 'dist/ui';
const pkg = JSON.parse(await readFile('package.json', 'utf8'));

async function build() {
  await mkdir(OUT, { recursive: true });

  const files = (await readdir(SRC))
    .filter((f) => f.endsWith('.js'))
    .sort();

  const parts = [`/* pipewatch ui v${pkg.version} */`];
  for (const f of files) {
    const code = await readFile(path.join(SRC, f), 'utf8');
    parts.push(`// --- ${f} ---\n${code}`);
  }

  await writeFile(path.join(OUT, 'app.js'), parts.join('\n'));
  await copyFile(path.join(SRC, 'index.html'), path.join(OUT, 'index.html'));
  await copyFile(path.join(SRC, 'style.css'), path.join(OUT, 'style.css'));

  console.log(`built ${files.length} files -> ${OUT}/app.js`);
}

build().catch((err) => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

I can't give you an honest kWh or CO2 figure. Multiplying a guess by users and days would just make up a number. The direction is clear, though: expect no measurable saving.

**What minification changes:** fewer bytes to send, and a little less text for the browser to parse. For small bundles, the bytes are the main effect.

**Measured reference:** in Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, 50 cold page loads of a small booking page used ~837 J with unminified JS and CSS and ~822 J with an esbuild-minified build. That is about 2% less, which is within measurement noise. Your bundle is in the same small range.

**Why your case is even smaller:** the dashboard is served from localhost. So the byte saving, which is the real benefit on real networks, is almost zero on every page load. Bytes only cross a real network when someone installs or updates the npm package. Then the tarball gets a bit smaller, once per install.

For the report, the honest line is "minification brings no measurable energy saving for this tool." Don't put a calculated kWh value there.

**How to check it yourself:**
1. Bytes: compare `gzip -c dist/ui/app.js | wc -c` before and after, and the package size from `npm pack --dry-run`.
2. Energy: write a Green Metrics Tool usage scenario that starts `pipewatch serve` and loads the dashboard many times in a browser. Run it with both builds on the same machine. If the difference is inside the run-to-run variation, then that is your result.

If you still want a smaller package, the change is small. Use `banner` so the version comment survives. Minification strips normal comments.

```javascript
import { transform } from 'esbuild';

// in build(), instead of the version line in `parts` and the plain writeFile:
const { code } = await transform(parts.join('\n'), {
  loader: 'js',
  minify: true,
  banner: `/* pipewatch ui v${pkg.version} */`,
});
await writeFile(path.join(OUT, 'app.js'), code);
```

The `// --- file ---` markers will be gone from the output. Add `sourcemap: true` if you still need to debug the dashboard.

```js
// pipeline-monitor.js
