  h1, h2, h3, .cta {
    font-family: inherit;
  }
  h1, h2 { font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.2; }
  h3 { font-weight: 600; }
  .hero { padding: 6rem 1.5rem; background: #e8f3ec; }
  .hero p { max-width: 38rem; font-style: italic; }
  .cta {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.8rem 1.6rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: #1f7a4d;
    color: #fff;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  ```
- **Impact:** reduces network and client E on every cold load. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks of 30 cold page loads, six local TTF files (~4 MB) used ~556 J, two Latin-subset WOFF2 files (~55 KB) ~488 J, and the system font stack ~410 J. That is about 26% less than TTF and about 16% less than WOFF2.

#### [Low] If the brand font is a must
- **Where:** the font setup as a whole
- **Issue:** the page will now look like each visitor's OS font. If the design needs Montserrat, keep it small.
- **Fix:** use one or two Latin-subset WOFF2 files, for example Montserrat 600 and 700 for headings only, and keep the system stack for body text. That was the next best option in the same measurement, at ~12% less than TTF.

